449 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 282 0 R
/E (This chapter provides background information on autism spectrum disorder and reviews related research in autism behavior analysis. It discusses existing approaches and technologies, highlighting their limitations and the research gap addressed by this project.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
450 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 282 0 R
/E (Autism Spectrum Disorder is characterized by a wide range of behavioral manifestations, including repetitive movements, atypical gestures, and restricted patterns of activity. These behaviors vary significantly among individuals, making automated detection a challenging task.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
451 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 282 0 R
/E (Understanding these behavioral characteristics is essential for designing computer vision systems capable of capturing meaningful patterns from visual data.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
452 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 282 0 R
/E (Several studies have explored the use of technology to support autism screening, including wearable sensors, eye-tracking systems, and video-based analysis. Among these approaches, video analysis offers a non-invasive and cost-effective solution capable of capturing natural behavior in real-world environments.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
453 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 282 0 R
/E (Computer vision enables machines to interpret visual information from images and videos. Deep learning models, particularly convolutional neural networks \(CNNs\), have shown strong performance in tasks such as object detection, activity recognition, and motion analysis.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
454 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 282 0 R
/E (6)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
455 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 283 0 R
/T (2.5 Object Detection for Behavioral Analysis)
/E (2.5 Object Detection for Behavioral Analysis)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
456 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 283 0 R
/E (Object detection models aim to localize and classify objects or regions of interest within an image. When applied to behavioral analysis, these models can be trained to detect specific body movements or gestures associated with particular behaviors.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
457 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 283 0 R
/T (2.6 YOLO-Based Detection Models)
/E (2.6 YOLO-Based Detection Models)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
458 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 283 0 R
/E (YOLO \(You Only Look Once\) is a single-stage object detection model designed for real-time performance. Its ability to process images in a single forward pass makes it suitable for applications requiring fast inference and immediate feedback.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
459 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 283 0 R
/T (2.7 Research Gap)
/E (2.7 Research Gap)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
460 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 283 0 R
/E (Despite the increasing interest in applying computer vision techniques to autism-related behavior detection, one of the most significant challenges remains the availability and quality of suitable datasets. Most existing studies rely on relatively large, well-curated datasets collected under controlled conditions, which are often not publicly available or difficult to reproduce.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
461 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 283 0 R
/E (In practice, autism-related behavioral data is scarce, heterogeneous, and challenging to annotate, particularly when sourced from real-world videos. Variations in recording conditions, camera angles, child behavior, and environmental context further complicate the data collection process.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
462 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 283 0 R
/E (This project addresses this research gap by investigating the feasibility of training a real-time behavior detection model under limited and imperfect data conditions. Significant effort was dedicated to manual annotation, data cleaning, and augmentation to compensate for data scarcity. By focusing on data preparation and robustness rather than ideal dataset assumptions, the project reflects realistic constraints encountered in practical applications.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
