583 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 293 0 R
/E (The primary dataset used in this project is the Self-Stimulatory Behaviours in the Wild for Autism Diagnosis \(SSBD\) dataset. The dataset consists of 75 videos, each approximately 90 seconds long, depicting children exhibiting autism-related repetitive behaviors such as arm flapping, head banging, and spinning in natural, uncontrolled environments.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
584 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 293 0 R
/E (Due to the sensitive nature of autism-related data, the dataset is limited in size and exhibits significant variability in lighting conditions, backgrounds, camera angles, and subject movement.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
585 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 293 0 R
/E (One of the main challenges encountered in this project was the limited availability of high-quality, annotated video data related to autism spectrum disorder. Publicly available datasets are scarce, and existing data often lack detailed annotations suitable for object detection tasks.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
586 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 293 0 R
/E (To address this limitation, additional data samples were carefully collected by the author from publicly accessible sources, strictly adhering to ethical guidelines, privacy regulations, and data usage policies. No private or identifiable personal information was used. These supplementary samples were integrated solely to enhance model learning and robustness.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
